  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 318 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nDry conditions remains likely through the first half of the upcoming \nweek. Upper ridging across the western CONUS should flatten some \nwith general northwesterly flow persisting. Moisture will remain \nbottled up along the Gulf Coast with westerly low-level flow within \na dry airmass supporting above normal temperatures. Daily max \ntemperature values at both KLBF and KVTN appear set to exceed the \n75th percentile values in each site's respective climatology for Max \nand Min temperatures. Confidence remains high in dry conditions \nthrough the middle of the week for most locations.\n\nA new wrinkle in the coming forecast is a frontal boundary expected \nto move into the area around the late Wednesday/Thursday timeframe. \nA northern stream disturbance should drag another cool front south \ninto the area. While this is unlikely to support a noteworthy \nairmass change, the boundary should stall in the area with \nincreasing kinematics into the evening supporting rain and \nthunderstorm potential. Deterministic solutions differ on timing and \nplacement thus this remains somewhat low confidence, however, recent \nensemble guidance shows increasing QPF probabilities for central and \neastern Nebraska. Output from the NAM, GFS, and ECWMF all paint at \nleast light QPF but differ on placement, timing, and magnitudes. For \nnow, have maintained a dry forecast with newly introduced PoPs \nfocused over Knox/Cedar Counties immediately to our east. Greater \nsupport for rainfall chances and cooler temperatures should arrive \nthis weekend as a much more formidable trough dives southeast across \nthe Pacific Northwest. Southwesterly flow prior to arrival of this \nupper-level disturbance will allow for greater moisture quality to \nestablish itself and should support increased rain potential. An \nintroduction of cooler air from the northwest bears watching for its \npotential to introduce our first shot at wintry weather but for now \nthis appears likely to remain over western South Dakota. \nTemperatures should fall through the weekend into the following week \nbut to what magnitude remains unclear as NBM temperature spreads \nremain quite large.",
